gigactl (1.2.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Privileged safety foundation (M1, software-tested, not hardware-
    validated): lifetime controller lease, exact DMI allowlist (GIGABYTE
    G5 GE / BIOS FB06, G6 KF by model), explicit opt-in raw EC transport,
    thermal supervision of manual and curve modes, requested-vs-observed
    duty reconciliation with bounded ramps, independent per-fan firmware
    handback with pending retry, async polkit with superseded-request
    invalidation, control-loop watchdog, and suspend/resume reconciliation
    with validated saved-state restore.
  * CLI write guards mirror the daemon allowlist exactly; thermal guard
    treats an unavailable GPU like the daemon does.
  * GUI: a tray host that registers after the --tray grace timer hides
    the fallback window again; fan requests are serialized per owner and
    curves keep firmware-escape and thermal safety.
  * Fedora: dedicated gigactld_t SELinux domain replacing the broad init_t
    module, isolated root-only daemon launcher, hardened unit (systemd
    watchdog, runtime directory with preserved owner lock, start limits),
    and clean-committed-source RPM builds that refuse dirty trees.
  * Release validation: daemon/GUI/CLI/packaging suites green; RPM built
    from a clean tree, installed and upgrade-tested on Fedora 44.

 -- gigactl contributors <gigactl@example.invalid>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:47:46 +0700

gigactl (1.1.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Gigabyte G5 GE verified on hardware (fans and single-zone RGB,
    BIOS FB06): both fans driven and handed back, keyboard colour set.
  * Fedora package (fedora/gigactl.spec): ships a SELinux policy module,
    because Fedora kernels build the EC without the ec_sys debugfs
    interface. Without ec_sys the daemon fails closed; raw /dev/port
    access requires an explicit administrator opt-in.
  * CPU turbo boost toggle in the app (intel_pstate no_turbo), with a
    dedicated polkit action; the daemon reports the live sysfs state.
  * Daemon failsafe: repeated tick failures now hand the fans back to
    firmware instead of leaving a stale manual duty in place.
  * Known issue: tuned profiles carrying boost=0 (e.g. tuned-ppd power
    profile switches, AC/battery transitions) can turn turbo back off
    after the app turned it on; the app shows the live state.

 -- gigactl contributors <gigactl@example.invalid>  Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0700

gigactl (1.0.2) noble; urgency=medium

  * The app now starts on a machine that has never run a cairo-drawing GTK
    application: the package was missing the PyGObject-to-cairo bridge, so
    GigaControl closed immediately with "Couldn't find foreign struct converter
    for 'cairo.Context'". Reported in #25.

  * An Embedded Controller that refuses to be written now says so, and says
    what to do about it. Where fan and backlight commands used to fail with
    "[Errno 22] Invalid argument" — a kernel refusal reported as though the
    daemon had rejected it — gigactl now explains that the kernel's EC
    interface was loaded read-only and gives the command that fixes it.
    Temperatures and fan speeds keep being reported throughout, because
    reading was never the part that failed. Reported in #25.

  * A fan profile that is not reaching the hardware no longer looks like one
    that is. "gfan status" used to announce the chosen profile while the fans
    sat at whatever the firmware was doing; it now reports the profile and,
    separately, that nothing is being applied. The app window says the same in
    its banner, and stops offering controls as though they would work.

  * The hardware support table distinguishes machines verified here from
    machines users have reported working (#24, #25), and docs/PROTOCOL.md
    records the unreproduced G5 KF register claims from #24 as open questions
    rather than findings.

 -- khalil Smairi <smairio98@gmail.com>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 18:21:30 +0100

gigactl (1.0.1) noble; urgency=low

  * Add manual pages for gfan(1), gkbd(1), gigactl-gui(1) and gigactld(8),
    clearing the only warnings lintian still reported. gigactld(8) documents the
    D-Bus interface, the polkit model, both Embedded Controller backends and the
    firmware-restore failsafe.

 -- khalil Smairi <smairio98@gmail.com>  Sat, 25 Jul 2026 09:45:00 +0100

gigactl (1.0.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * GigaControl Desktop v1: first packaged release.
  * Ships the gigactld daemon (system-bus API, polkit-gated writes, fan curve
    engine, keyboard backlight persistence across boot and resume), the GTK4
    GigaControl GUI with its tray indicator and login autostart, and the gfan
    and gkbd command line tools.
  * Supersedes the standalone gkbd-restore.service and its system-sleep hook:
    the daemon now owns keyboard restore, from /var/lib/gigactl/state.json.
  * gfan and gkbd route through the daemon whenever it is running, so they need
    neither root nor ec_probe in normal use, and cannot compete with it for the
    Embedded Controller. Without a daemon they write the EC directly as before,
    which does still need ec_probe (nbfc-linux, not in the apt archive).

 -- khalil Smairi <smairio98@gmail.com>  Fri, 24 Jul 2026 22:00:00 +0100
